wer-field/id #uuid "435b445b-a1d3-5c7e-bda2-9e456fd12252" :answer-field/key "![Diagram III](images/57/57d69ec94f2cdfb8e20d959240983309aac60814ac0f50fba2f7381f04da2429.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475055} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4459c66-a2f3-5445-90f0-ae904bdcc170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668989} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ccc508-a7f2-5577-ab13-670537fed342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668990}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668990} :answer-field/id #uuid "3588ac9c-129e-5d9d-8648-01c928c71177" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61609b84-fa99-5574-b117-b0c4a018b4cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-110456" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the whole plane ${R}^{2}$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb17f7e3-269e-5625-a111-f4d4af48c417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474272} {:answer/id #uuid "31d11665-b6a6-55f9-a8b1-68377f92906b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474273}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474272} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1b2942f-e716-57f1-88e3-40e92d8e3656" :answer-field/key "$A=\\{⟨1+t,t-1⟩\\,:\\,t\\in R\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475062} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ad710ac-351e-551c-9aa3-eb471c6ba7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474274} {:answer/id #uuid "ff323ad8-0d05-55f5-9ec9-2dd89adb86b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474275}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474274} :answer-field/id #uuid "189d1c47-187f-5ca5-b080-65e61257620b" :answer-field/key "$B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:xy=0\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475063} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b68cef7-62b5-5d0d-8003-74ec4afeca09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474276} {:answer/id #uuid "e73f1446-6ae4-598e-9a58-e6999a487413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474277} :answer-field/id #uuid "58329b77-3f5c-5c03-892f-99af51f6bc0b" :answer-field/key "$C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=0\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "784a3447-3ec5-5b3c-aa05-c4909c41863a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110763" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1+t \\\\ t-1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}t+1 &=0 \\\\ t-1 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe see that the system has no solution. So, $0$ is not in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace. Indeed, both $a=⟨1,0⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1⟩$ belong to $B$ but \n\n$$\na+b=⟨1,0⟩+⟨0,1⟩=⟨1,1⟩\n$$\n\n \ndoes not satisfy the condition $xy=0$ since $1\\cdot 1\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It represents a line (the $y$-axis), and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=0$ since $0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=0\\}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426534 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe8bd27-3332-5e19-ab00-90bec8a7d8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474430} {:answer/id #uuid "853cd590-6caa-56b3-a59f-0675ccb0e749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474431} :answer-field/id #uuid "563623e9-779c-5c19-a3d5-42d130d33d79" :answer-field/key "![Diagram I](images/ab/abe45d3a709d0f7e11aacecfbb1352c2f8f25030bf46b41eb364fd8841db0a90.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475141} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2123dbe4-7024-5943-a1bb-063a94890da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474432} {:answer/id #uuid "d93c4474-f728-51c9-9ef3-970a99af9b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474433}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474432} :answer-field/id #uuid "725aa56a-3ba3-5d2e-bc80-310e770ce2e5" :answer-field/key "![Diagram II](images/27/27e05f375ca7a2ee532ad68b68fcb69f3bb1b3e737d69aa510b581e0f28645e4.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475142} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027fb362-49b2-5c99-954e-f219c00ba6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474434} {:answer/id #uuid "0dcb4af6-96b5-543a-b142-134acab8924c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474435}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474435} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d76cd3a-8ed8-5ff4-ac05-f9a94b840c44" :answer-field/key "![Diagram III](images/5e/5ee1919aabad947c1637b2cbee49ab7150ac56d7ff9ea3a21bd915d1a262c363.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d538e48-6d40-5139-9e4a-4a2045c240b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-158073" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagrams I and III only"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f3871a9-6e60-5fd0-923a-baae81d1dcc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474266} {:answer/id #uuid "08e41dcc-4096-52b0-b648-56bc1c24d9e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474267}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474267} :answer-field/id #uuid "8deb3772-23f8-57f0-9654-27f663eb5b79" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}A=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\x-y+z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475059} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a735ecf6-9831-50b7-9118-50ebb21e7bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474268} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9a6da5-190a-5a0b-9e24-d7c79706a2e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474269} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0e48cf1-2e8f-594c-98aa-3c8a0ee95a28" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}B=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475060} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be9edb74-2397-5ca7-b83f-9320c8ba3023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474270} {:answer/id #uuid "72e5d407-78c6-57eb-916b-f720eba7787b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474271}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474270} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c506523-20a6-5d70-a10c-c4a2e49bed4c" :answer-field/key "$C=\\{⟨0,0,1⟩\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c109cc3b-b729-5d92-be15-5107dfc714ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-110759" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y+z=0$ since $0-0+0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. This subset has only one element, the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩,$ which is a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. This subset has only one element, the vector $⟨0,0,1⟩,$ so it does not contain the zero vector.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}A=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\x-y+z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered};\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}B=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426536 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88fce6b8-72cb-5526-bb0a-0025565c3bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474260} {:answer/id #uuid "8990ca53-4eaf-5938-924a-f1df2655d3f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474260} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea7d197d-2ddf-5c30-8a44-e8e2ca3897d3" :answer-field/key "![Diagram I](images/02/023ae911f93a7464761700a5e31759a6d81db86eb972300b9dc715b8199dddd4.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475056} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "909aea3b-2b52-5b46-8274-8eee93af3e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474262} {:answer/id #uuid "899ec79d-8add-5b1c-891d-d649234a580e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474262} :answer-field/id #uuid "78b749fe-74f2-5f84-9485-28c26e1aba6e" :answer-field/key "![Diagram II](images/df/df8564bcd4bf3b1c6327b79c98fdaaa28374da4a7da1e239ec99e65f69370823.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4399d37c-7fb5-57ea-8911-bc4290f14f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474264} {:answer/id #uuid "7629f35b-fff6-5124-ac59-0a11bbdc38ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474265}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474265} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8e5aaaf-be26-5099-8d37-7e8745a6b18f" :answer-field/key "![Diagram III](images/09/09d0ceb2462ed85cd7a6e6319ac585e56a5c37adc14302eb51b923b1f193b72a.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4fcfc53-7279-50f5-90f1-bd8c9662d8f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-110599" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram III only"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20671737-03a7-5430-89bf-040b752377e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-4,3,31⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429805} {:answer/id #uuid "6c5b071d-27ac-57b7-af6d-728934e76426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,5,31⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429808} {:answer/id #uuid "745f1be0-1559-5f77-9632-8cdde3498554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-5,3,31⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429809} {:answer/id #uuid "cb172289-6398-5303-bbd8-83b5e2e9bc68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-5,7,31⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429811} {:answer/id #uuid "dea73573-73e0-539b-8d97-0a1d8ac2f0eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-5,3,27⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429812}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429809} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b496fd-8c3a-561b-bf53-11d88d290ff5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c3b23dc-4bcb-57b2-b0fc-dd67734d3e36" :question/math-academy-id "q-128493" :question/problem "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 17 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+s\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,t,\\,s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨2,-7,1⟩$ and $w=⟨3,5,0⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5i+3j+31k \\\\  & =⟨-5,3,31⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-5,3,31⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076c0841-49e0-5efa-932b-cea12451f354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-2,-2,5⟩+t⟨-3,4,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429935} {:answer/id #uuid "36f17219-1319-57b9-b736-3daca3fe9f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-2,-2,5⟩+t⟨-2,4,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429936} {:answer/id #uuid "42182090-ca90-5aff-919b-f97f28d17f45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-3,-2,7⟩+t⟨-3,4,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429937} {:answer/id #uuid "a639639c-9709-5ea4-b9d7-eed8e4f97ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429938} {:answer/id #uuid "dd4f3967-0094-5670-b34b-c4d617495978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-2,4,3⟩+t⟨-3,4,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429939}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429937} :answer-field/id #uuid "918bca49-2085-5b3c-9bbd-1ed60948277f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e59ecbbf-3ac2-51fb-8da8-83763d787a9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71033" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $D(1,0,-2),\\,E(-2,-2,5)$, and $F(-2,4,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =⟨-2,-2,5⟩-⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-2,7⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =⟨-2,4,3⟩-⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,4,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $d=⟨1,0,-2⟩$ of the point $D,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =d+s\\overset{\\to }{DE}+t\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\ r & =⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-3,-2,7⟩+t⟨-3,4,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ad1af7-5e4b-5b23-8b4a-240b1732cda6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433480} {:answer/id #uuid "5092a904-7fb7-5b45-afc3-5d2f76eb301d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433482} {:answer/id #uuid "546003b5-05e8-5b2c-b100-ec0f104c1909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ne " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433483} {:answer/id #uuid "a147a2b2-ac26-5864-a695-452d2ee464cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433487} {:answer/id #uuid "d48d53eb-2eb5-59fd-bc2b-f89816dbd891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433487} :answer-field/id #uuid "6778b0be-3214-5289-bc42-c4d9931ed8b3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433489} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41080398-b2ba-56b8-9658-937f33ed7f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433481} {:answer/id #uuid "64c0d4ca-3551-5246-9474-bf87dc44f240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433484} {:answer/id #uuid "76d2c0ab-aee8-5245-b31a-1656f6a34d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433485} {:answer/id #uuid "a114218b-7eb8-53d8-acac-162934598611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433485} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebda1f69-fee2-52dd-85c4-c20bfd2e3b58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee4392fe-5c34-5631-8fcd-93d0be346505" :question/math-academy-id "q-181693" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f9807dae43519dbd9407130c51cb1473a36c46a42d8c433d9b4d88edb4e382d.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,2{y}^{2}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *to the right* of the curve $x=2{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>2{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the curve is dashed), the inequality is strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x>2{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42472aa9-7d67-5ed4-b98a-d5c155583284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474810} {:answer/id #uuid "50eed6d6-c97a-50b5-8b9a-6beba0cf0a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474811}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474811} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca653d76-a75a-51a4-80dd-bb7659f5015c" :answer-field/key "$A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=0\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475331} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bd6c4a2-af1f-5698-a7b3-9ffe19512167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474812} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9541a5-f46e-5e6b-afb1-b87a0a138a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474813}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474813} :answer-field/id #uuid "4675387e-3ac9-5537-9ed6-f89ff87766bb" :answer-field/key "$B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=3x\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475332} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f901af94-c98d-501a-b234-ab17178d0c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474814} {:answer/id #uuid "336c0586-f08c-5ea5-a8bb-a690031bc9ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474815}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474814} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe925f85-a920-529c-848e-b9bb308c481f" :answer-field/key "$C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=2\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f1ed82d-67ec-55b7-826d-30733bc2c88f" :question/math-academy-id "q-35575" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $y=0$ since $0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $y=3x$ since $0=3\\cdot 0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=2$ since $0\\ne 2.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=0\\}$; $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=3x\\}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37d331a3-d216-56b1-b1e8-c1c659788885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445208} {:answer/id #uuid "b1bdd0cd-8af9-54f9-913d-1f9c797b10c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445213} {:answer/id #uuid "c7324eda-c77e-5439-bf52-4a39226f0238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445215} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e7ce1b-ea58-5c77-a5f2-354c10202f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445216} {:answer/id #uuid "e17bf533-f3b3-5889-bc5d-63042f883173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445215} :answer-field/id #uuid "df160bc6-de12-5b0e-ab4a-f86e448d27ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "685229d2-34f8-5173-a67b-785ec23ea6ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-127876" :question/problem "A nonzero vector $⟨a,\\,b,\\,0⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨2,-1,3⟩=17.$ What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨2,-1,3⟩=17$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-1,3⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2{t}_{1}-{t}_{2}+3{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =2{t}_{1}+3{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},\\,2{t}_{1}+3{t}_{3},\\,{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the third component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{3}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{1}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{1}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨1,\\,2,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c0119a-d6a0-5768-b521-97fbdb1b4a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478654} {:answer/id #uuid "5f082dfc-3b85-5a93-bc09-af1e742628ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478693} {:answer/id #uuid "65dcc751-70ac-5835-895f-9afe1c6981be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478708} {:answer/id #uuid "9290ba2f-84e6-50ba-bd20-50955e2e64ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478796} {:answer/id #uuid "db8d4f50-3033-5b61-9824-d94717ad9840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478904}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478708} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc384e98-6294-5707-b8e0-2ed99c0215ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d0c1517-fefb-518c-9505-969066e0bce6" :question/math-academy-id "q-110601" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:4x+5y-z=0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $4x+5y-z=0$ since $4\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ since ${0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. This subset has only one element, the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩,$ which is a special subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154b8691-57de-5b5e-86f8-c31acc6d30e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478587} {:answer/id #uuid "62d73fd1-aa7f-5ec7-8f6d-743894ccf613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478702} {:answer/id #uuid "759885ae-9ef9-5c93-ab20-c1fe824077c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478734} {:answer/id #uuid "c3271208-f908-5d5d-adf8-766e9fc2f767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478874} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e434bd-13dd-5cf1-bb32-448ac1fcc0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478911}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478911} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c014ca5-93c9-54e8-95bc-87d740d016c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ada9b840-b530-5081-8048-c09da4df32f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-110606" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:3x+4y=1\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x+3y=0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ consists only of the vector $⟨0,0⟩$, which is a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $3x+4y=1$ since $3\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It also represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x+3y=0$ since $0+3\\cdot 0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "426d2f86-6e44-5b72-8222-a1d8c826de45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,-5,7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489666} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee143c1-2d22-5a6b-8226-53337f0c9aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,1,7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489742} {:answer/id #uuid "81af70fa-ea19-54cc-83e0-b0a21fab95e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-34,-22,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489809} {:answer/id #uuid "87ba8eac-d335-511b-94ef-29242597cc39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,-5,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489824} {:answer/id #uuid "a52411c7-786b-5482-9881-e7a8a452b864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨36,22,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489882}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489882} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bf0e22c-7173-584a-ae1b-a7c0bd01e586" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7340caf5-7f57-5ed9-b918-72cd124f498f" :question/math-academy-id "q-70975" :question/problem "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,8,-1⟩+t⟨-1,1,7⟩+s⟨3,-5,1⟩,\\,t,\\,s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-1,1,7⟩$ and $w=⟨3,-5,1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =36i+22j+2k \\\\  & =⟨36,22,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨36,22,2⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b587818-a1ff-517e-a413-a2f1bc765f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594855} {:answer/id #uuid "c74b3f5b-448e-52a1-a3fd-d84f98d190fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595493} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b91f5b-43c6-5b4b-bfcc-17aa3be17b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595572} {:answer/id #uuid "e1dd6b56-48cd-5292-a008-bed7d994c3d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595577} {:answer/id #uuid "fef0f413-22c6-590f-a67c-83fb11b6f515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595686}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595493} :answer-field/id #uuid "497610eb-e3bd-58c2-aa6b-b130f8df245e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ef295de-639b-5c86-b5f1-6da2555ecede" :question/math-academy-id "q-110469" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b120810f9c4010d7d59014b85462a5f251a4e2efa7b41f1324a4297bb3dee989.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2db71ba7-113c-510b-bf77-cf532d597605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594968} {:answer/id #uuid "40202440-4c19-5337-9699-11a11bf60db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595023} {:answer/id #uuid "69c4af85-5254-5a25-9c43-687ad88e08f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595163} {:answer/id #uuid "88c96c7f-7086-556d-9a68-2fc3c1c6af3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595283} {:answer/id #uuid "92ba791f-1575-513e-92c3-5ce6c360fefb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595023} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce746e9d-d73a-51bf-82f2-af7f6dbf43c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7aa95c2-337f-59c4-8f44-816b62e77009" :question/math-academy-id "q-110491" :question/problem "![](images/df/dffdfde8c206cafe12087ed8e02d14b51a22932e83003351d9476ab84109c919.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace.  In this case we have that the vectors $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ lie inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\na+b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis not in the subset.\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the given subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3280af5d-0b23-57a6-83a3-60eb511ca901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594981} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5c3b50-121e-5349-a6a8-a335831c3652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595014} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8a9f99-2446-5c84-9222-3b1d06eaf2c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595071} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd592fd-3af9-5735-88b6-21c53e9d65ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595465} {:answer/id #uuid "f0384eaf-3bc7-5eeb-b458-89bbdc9b144e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595071} :answer-field/id #uuid "53a40651-4e93-5464-9c2b-3ce087be0581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e14602fb-9502-5e8a-8b45-d6f709e56eaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-158011" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a3dfc27c8cae5db8bccd14048366531e2aa556802943d464ea12b1568a8ba380.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace since in does not contain the zero-vector.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ab3918a-6e85-5111-93b8-cf7febf0cb71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595169} {:answer/id #uuid "7de64b36-0006-5348-99cd-c36c9f673551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595235} {:answer/id #uuid "82c5921e-a0fa-5096-8b8d-592e74413f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595261} {:answer/id #uuid "940a93ac-865f-5a5b-8fbb-7d0469a8dee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595330} {:answer/id #uuid "dc300f67-0f2a-5ede-8249-25ef1ae9f530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595261} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d53e0c2-a525-5054-bbca-4c5248aa5277" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fff70113-5175-56d9-a218-bf9304e3e90a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74256" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{t\\cdot ⟨2,-4,1⟩\\,:\\,t\\in R\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:xzy=1\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}>0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the zero vector, $0.$ This can be verified by taking $t=0$, since \n\n$$\n0\\cdot ⟨2,-4,1⟩=⟨0,0,0⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, $0$ lies in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $xyz=1$ since $0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}>0$ since ${0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}=0.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7cf352-c4c7-5746-926f-e0cda773dff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607818} {:answer/id #uuid "5efc3cb7-b270-5eac-b28b-3519ed912f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607927} {:answer/id #uuid "5f362b0f-220a-5686-985e-0d1dda4be5d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607928} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e97e75-b8f2-5af3-a31a-33bc1b4c4374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608026} {:answer/id #uuid "decb37ab-3fe7-5bb2-92a6-344068a70171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608050}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608026} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc17dcb4-ea91-5742-8daa-89740609377d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5986a52f-1816-5c27-a154-2e9b7894f16b" :question/math-academy-id "q-34321" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x+y=1\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=0\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=y=0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a line, but it is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $x+y=1$ since $0+0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=0$ since $0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ consists only of the vector $⟨0,0⟩$, which is a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2def5c7e-a331-55b6-b464-e61fe5acfec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607854} {:answer/id #uuid "41f79be9-1da4-58b7-a884-b011bb967b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607889} {:answer/id #uuid "43b14e7d-5fd4-5d16-b474-345e36dc747a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607891} {:answer/id #uuid "687aa851-d627-5587-9064-c2f5b282298f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607936} {:answer/id #uuid "9010dd36-94c2-50c7-9a47-38ca16cb4626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607974}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607974} :answer-field/id #uuid "b863de7a-b109-5713-801f-182d8557ecff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba81c1f5-23e1-5ab9-940f-dda78b88260d" :question/math-academy-id "q-34326" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3b9a0be13d351fd52724dc38e1d80fd64d87a3f2fd56f9ae8cc96b3b56d6fd89.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.  \n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. There are vectors $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But, \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f094dc0-8820-522f-b49d-3d076fe73196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15i+9j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619209} {:answer/id #uuid "795db67d-cc67-5a22-ba63-34e44c9b0ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15i-9j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619360} {:answer/id #uuid "7f424fc2-5c22-5040-a574-2ff4413f3fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9i+15j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619369} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a61a0a-d6e9-5f6c-95d8-82545c07b575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7i+9j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619503} {:answer/id #uuid "ee123eaa-a376-5d21-97f9-e4efdf56cfc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15i+3j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619558}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619209} :answer-field/id #uuid "b31ecc54-9265-5182-99b4-fe2cd0d4c4ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49ac232e-6de6-51a0-b147-f48cc384e577" :question/math-academy-id "q-19766" :question/problem "Find $(-a+2b)\\times (3c)$, if $a\\times c=i+j$ and $b\\times c=3i+2j.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use distributive property:\n\n$$\n(-a+2b)\\times (3c)=(-a)\\times (3c)+(2b)\\times (3c)\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-a)\\times (3c)+(2b)\\times (3c) & =(-1\\cdot 3)(a\\times c)+(2\\cdot 3)(b\\times c) \\\\  & =-3(a\\times c)+6(b\\times c)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $a\\times c$ and $b\\times c,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3(a\\times c)+6(b\\times c) & =-3(i+j)+6(3i+2j) \\\\  & =-3i-3j+18i+12j \\\\  & =15i+9j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91f2c468-ea86-59a5-9fca-28363d183fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619398}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619398} :answer-field/id #uuid "00e0e0f7-a680-5d16-b241-0b56d82296e1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619592} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e16a671a-411e-5ce5-a316-7d75818b350d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619536}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619536} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f241d4d-6404-5eb2-8ca4-128cff9ea157" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619597} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49faa7c9-f9a6-5920-b871-4e0b61362064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619281} :answer-field/id #uuid "b351a8d0-ba82-5dba-9898-9c2e3b95ccd3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bc2009e-7995-556d-a2d0-9f212711c9de" :question/math-academy-id "q-280369" :question/problem "Let $c\\times a=-2i+j+3k$ and $c\\times b=-i+4j.$ Find $2c\\times (3a+b).$\n\n$2c\\times (3a+b)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$j\\,\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n$k$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use distributive property:\n\n$$\n2c\\times (3a+b)=2c\\times (3a)+2c\\times b\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c\\times (3a)+2c\\times b & =6(c\\times a)+2(c\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $c\\times a$ and $c\\times b,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(c\\times a)+2(c\\times b) & =6(-2i+j+3k)+2(-i+4j) \\\\  & =-12i+6j+18k-2i+8j \\\\  & =-14\\,i+14\\,j+18\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "453090ee-8a9d-50a9-98f1-61d2710b87da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619273}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619273} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a9aac80-702e-579a-aac2-ae20f9c87125" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619601} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a482656-2fa6-53a3-a6f3-8cabad597f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619335}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619335} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f67844c-c7cd-565d-9d1b-7baa80a68983" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619620} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4c550f1-a96a-5bb8-8438-d61faef920e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619494}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619494} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4e4480c-9709-5cea-9be4-d0e99f66c6b7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cab06a75-19db-5a30-a8e2-e30108934976" :question/math-academy-id "q-280838" :question/problem "Let $b\\times a=-i+4j-2k.$ Find $3a\\times (-2a+b).$\n\n$3a\\times (-2a+b)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$j\\,\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n$k$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\na\\times a=0.\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =-b\\times a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n3a\\times (-2a+b)=(3a)\\times (-2a)+(3a)\\times b\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3a)\\times (-2a)+(3a)\\times b & =(3\\cdot -2)(a\\times a)+3(a\\times b) \\\\  & =-6(a\\times a)+3(a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $a\\times a=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6(a\\times a)+3(a\\times b) & =-6(0)+3(a\\times b) \\\\  & =3(a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substitute the given value for $a\\times b$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(a\\times b) & =3(i-4j+2k) \\\\  & =3i\\,+\\,-12j\\,+\\,6k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1022b8bf-b0c6-530f-a519-d12cada02fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186640367} {:answer/id #uuid "a635bf0d-63e0-57f2-8609-62ee741ec9b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,2,5⟩+s⟨1,\\frac{2}{3},3⟩+t⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186640549} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b63d04-f531-5e32-ae82-bf9fc24ccb64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,\\frac{2}{3},3⟩+s⟨-3,2,5⟩+t⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186640559} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb773d3-50e0-5f71-9a25-28056f49edc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩+s⟨-3,2,5⟩+t⟨1,\\frac{2}{3},3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186640613} {:answer/id #uuid "fec4ddb1-dc04-594d-b5c4-d1ed84addd04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,\\frac{2}{3},3⟩+s⟨-2,5,\\sqrt{6}⟩+t⟨1,-3,0⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186640649}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640559} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf91d12-136d-569d-aeb0-4c44b551b722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186640689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90811859-6ca0-5330-9179-86e6fc59408f" :question/math-academy-id "q-70979" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(1,\\frac{2}{3},3)$ and is parallel to the lines $r=⟨-2,5,\\sqrt{6}⟩+s⟨-3,2,5⟩$ and $r=⟨1,-3,0⟩+t⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨-3,2,5⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $Q(1,\\frac{2}{3},3)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $q=⟨1,\\frac{2}{3},3⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =q+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨1,\\frac{2}{3},3⟩+s⟨-3,2,5⟩+t⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172f4153-fd16-54ef-bb2c-3ac34b1da7a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨15,13,13⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186640374} {:answer/id #uuid "197f6fe6-b869-5358-a40e-e8237216751d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨13,0,13⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186640377} {:answer/id #uuid "1ac0cf79-9300-5fe6-9d72-26601d83fd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨13,13,15⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186640379} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b694c9-c173-5796-98fa-d8a0edd54f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨13,13,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186640564} {:answer/id #uuid "f4601a1f-dd24-5960-b047-795695596224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,13,13⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186640632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640564} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba997efa-2377-5f42-87a3-7566ffccfe27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186640707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d20fcedb-3514-53bb-8ae0-323a996facab" :question/math-academy-id "q-128498" :question/problem "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\nr=⟨-2,7,-6⟩+t⟨-2,2,3⟩+s⟨3,-3,2⟩,\\,t,\\,s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-2,2,3⟩$ and $w=⟨3,-3,2⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =13i+13j \\\\  & =⟨13,13,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨13,13,0⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a35a3872-94e4-55b9-a447-e02643ff0277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641334} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c475737-8719-548d-bd62-1f1be071060a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641500} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28280543-74b8-5d89-9d02-b690dcbfde9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641147}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641147} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd073a4-c010-505b-9c7c-a7d41cd34e90" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641507} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc02d9ee-0f35-5682-ba2a-f169af467241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641367} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7f58529-21a3-5f6b-8503-12db742a4ff7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "019336dc-8560-5e24-a42a-149abe08104a" :question/math-academy-id "q-330446" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-2,4,1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=3i-2j-4k.$\n\n$3x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-2,4,1⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨3,-2,-4⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-2,-4⟩ & =⟨-2,4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-2,-4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-2,-4⟩ & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-2,-4⟩ & =-18 \\\\ 3x-2y-4z & =-18 \\\\ 3x-2y-4z+18 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e113f230-d145-52c5-b750-c3ed1c28a53d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641426}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641426} :answer-field/id #uuid "00a55cc8-ad46-5d53-896c-2c3e98266c3d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641477} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fda217c-6921-5ae3-8008-ec61b128326d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641167}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641167} :answer-field/id #uuid "285accb2-da93-5e11-8082-73fec90693b8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641493} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5999ab50-000f-5e4a-82d4-ecd9ead89108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641227} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d9cbc0d-b80b-535c-95ca-f8bc5f33489f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04805a8e-6657-5251-b0c6-b947dd24b6ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-325977" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(3,1,-4)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=i-2j+5k.$\n\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨3,1,-4⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨1,-2,5⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,5⟩ & =⟨3,1,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-2,5⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,5⟩ & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-2,5⟩ & =-19 \\\\ x-2y+5z & =-19 \\\\ x-2y+5z+19 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c6f01f7-e4b7-5bdf-83ed-a10df2b45dde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641292}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641292} :answer-field/id #uuid "152ec7cf-f333-5692-88df-40b17c299d02" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641485} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8f801d2-60fd-52e8-a4ab-59994fecb352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641411} :answer-field/id #uuid "955b3084-b0bc-542d-9bbe-d1789ee150d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641542} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8d16f57-2cbe-5990-bbc4-9b2ba4448e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641341}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641341} :answer-field/id #uuid "aadf54e0-27e1-582b-8f98-3f660494e23d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b5eac9f-f03c-5b65-92ec-9107cd380eae" :question/math-academy-id "q-336238" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $5y-2z+15=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,$\n{{field-2}}\n,\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨0,5,-2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-2z+15 & =0 \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{2}y+\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $y=0$, we get $z=\\frac{15}{2}.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,\\frac{15}{2})$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,\\frac{15}{2}⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,\\frac{15}{2}⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,5,-2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641600 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d3ecee4-7cda-540b-824a-4241edbc862f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,5,4⟩=19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641260} {:answer/id #uuid "9da0a70d-c8c5-531a-8611-4e7c11cb8a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨4,5,2⟩=19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641319} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3e5149-a1e5-5064-8073-266f8c6dcead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,5,4⟩=22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641436} {:answer/id #uuid "ef46fdd1-8182-5f45-b56a-cac3aa6e5547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,4,5⟩=22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641442} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa76d78-d133-53ff-b78a-93fb8a645d94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨5,4,3⟩=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641319} :answer-field/id #uuid "6519e13a-b991-5af4-a39a-2c0bbad170a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d034b74-0bee-5a7f-91a8-3c09e07e1d07" :question/math-academy-id "q-127930" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(5,-1,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(7,-4,3)$ and $B(11,1,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨5,-1,2⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(7,-4,3)$ and $B(11,1,5)$, we can find a vector that is parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨11,1,5⟩-⟨7,-4,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,5,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨4,5,2⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,5,2⟩ & =⟨5,-1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨4,5,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,5,2⟩ & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641606 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29fda5b2-5312-58ee-a2c0-7d55a209748c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,7⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,2,0⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641151} {:answer/id #uuid "8373a17d-95ec-5641-b72e-1a67e056f602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,7⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,2,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641279} {:answer/id #uuid "c08fe42d-5df0-529e-9c49-2328bd1ceeef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-7⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,2,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641375} {:answer/id #uuid "c9eff9c0-cbbc-5bea-b04e-0eb39c9d2d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-7⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641390} {:answer/id #uuid "f4494ada-073e-5b3c-9758-68996f25e3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,7⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,1,7⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641450}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641279} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e7a291a-e955-5f79-bb1a-4dea34ab1986" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24444782-66fe-5647-9a82-ba3b2c4d4bf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-33924" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $x+2y-z+7=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨1,2,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y-z+7 & =0 \\\\ z & =x+2y+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=7.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,7)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,7⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,7⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,2,-1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d26e8f-a4a6-571d-a77f-47bbef70a327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-4y-z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641086} {:answer/id #uuid "28796087-1106-5e6e-8354-6ee0dbad4e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-5y-z+7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641148} {:answer/id #uuid "501fdb27-e2a5-5cf2-b475-377d1f48852b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+4y-z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641207} {:answer/id #uuid "85e9c1ae-4efb-5a95-9a2a-e8f6edd46541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+5y-z-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641283} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c9f13d-0e00-55fd-91a0-aade58c8e1f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+4y-z+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641458}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641207} :answer-field/id #uuid "9018c7f8-f8e3-5717-9f5f-5f1a58f08ba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61134754-897c-505d-8749-ccd6d3567c14" :question/math-academy-id "q-128373" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-1,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3+5t \\\\ y &=4t \\\\ z &=2-t\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨5,4,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨5,4,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨5,4,-1⟩ & =⟨1,-1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨5,4,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨5,4,-1⟩ & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨5,4,-1⟩ & =-1 \\\\ 5x+4y-z & =-1 \\\\ 5x+4y-z+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641630 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fe648db-49c7-539b-b857-b200d548360d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot (2i+j)=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641127} {:answer/id #uuid "4cddb044-c99b-568e-81b3-69a40fb7574b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot (-i+3k)=2i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641199} {:answer/id #uuid "5354827a-5966-5132-afb6-33c9534826cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot (-i+3k)=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641213} {:answer/id #uuid "acfb1d80-cf7a-528d-a00e-46508872c181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot (2i+j)=-i+3k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641349} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ff671b-b8ea-5ef5-b51b-f21ca2d420ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot (2i+3k)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641387}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641127} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e83b847-f49b-560b-b154-c85d45432399" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69ffc95e-5fee-561e-b86f-d21a2a25bf8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-32966" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(-1,0,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=2i+j.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=-i+3k$ and $n=2i+j$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot (2i+j) & =(-i+3k)\\cdot (2i+j) \\\\ r\\cdot (2i+j) & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641647 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1e78041-98b9-5a01-8e4e-bf922b86df38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641429}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641429} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f32f6db-1fca-5896-b5d6-6e39797f5931" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641490} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9095a417-eba2-5374-9da7-990da2cf97a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641303}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641303} :answer-field/id #uuid "53343447-1f88-5764-af65-ec0fec7c7813" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641509} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fb6afd6-11df-502b-a91e-4fd7620c36e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641164} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2b29dc8-f956-596f-9260-dfcec9c8a56d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a0d9c5c4-1de9-578a-968f-3c3a982946ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-336374" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(2,-1,0)$, $P(4,2,1)$ and $Q(0,1,-2).$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,-\\,y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨2,-1,0⟩,\\,p=⟨4,2,1⟩,$ and $q=⟨0,1,-2⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨4,2,1⟩-⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,1⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-2⟩-⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-8i+2j+10k \\\\  & =⟨-8,2,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-8,2,10⟩ & =⟨4,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-8,2,10⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-8,2,10⟩ & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-8,2,10⟩ & =-18 \\\\ -8x+2y+10z & =-18 \\\\ 8x-2y-10z-18 & =0 \\\\ 4x-y-5z-9 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641669 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09fcf429-208d-5708-ac85-8406a166fb56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+z+12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641091} {:answer/id #uuid "10d4bb23-1d73-5e6c-b7d0-8d2741eefa67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-3y+4z+12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641107} {:answer/id #uuid "2ffd2dca-3021-5698-a559-143af868070a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4y+2z-24=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641168} {:answer/id #uuid "3d20f161-fd0e-532c-b24f-cb6b9fd3c9a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-3y+4z+24=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641182} {:answer/id #uuid "a96e9519-cdea-508f-929f-eb87667a290b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+4y+12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641342}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641182} :answer-field/id #uuid "54149aaf-3d1f-56dd-8933-32c51699f488" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186641511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc908cf2-d6a9-5de6-a79c-61d3d3e0ae4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-70831" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,4,-2)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨2,0,-2⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨3,4,0⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-1,4,-2⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8i-6j+8k \\\\  & =⟨8,-6,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨8,-6,8⟩ & =⟨-1,4,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨8,-6,8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨8,-6,8⟩ & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨8,-6,8⟩ & =-48 \\\\ 8x-6y+8z & =-48 \\\\ 8x-6y+8z+48 & =0 \\\\ 4x-3y+4z+24 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186647799 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b2967ad-7772-574e-9779-4d3393b29000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-4y-z-13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186647247} {:answer/id #uuid "3604e5a9-975c-5518-8cb6-4985fae130f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-3y+z-9=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186647263} {:answer/id #uuid "99a52c15-c066-59b1-a6ed-2a8e090491e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y-4z-15=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186647448} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e11752-b2e9-5328-9542-ada3b325027a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-3y-z-11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186647467} {:answer/id #uuid "d831a16c-df75-5042-8063-a84e224acd1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+y-3z-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186647571}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186647467} :answer-field/id #uuid "efef9ca4-ee32-5128-8017-02e412b73bb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186647737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8fa95509-fe83-5cb2-8859-dd0fd081c77b" :question/math-academy-id "q-128460" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(1,-3,2)$, $P(2,-2,3)$ and $Q(4,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,-3,2⟩,\\,p=⟨2,-2,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨4,1,2⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,3⟩-⟨1,-3,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨4,1,2⟩-⟨1,-3,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4i+3j+k \\\\  & =⟨-4,3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,3,1⟩ & =⟨2,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,3,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,3,1⟩ & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,3,1⟩ & =-11 \\\\ -4x+3y+z & =-11 \\\\ -4x+3y+z+11 & =0 \\\\ 4x-3y-z-11 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0049d96f-dcfa-51fb-93a1-fad5f42e5b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,1,2⟩=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648749} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2c35ca-24fd-5da1-963a-0bfcda76ba06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648797} {:answer/id #uuid "6074b9b6-869f-5977-b48d-081432cd4a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,1,-3⟩=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648857} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0544f2-da93-5b96-8d9e-016b12447968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,1,2⟩=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648897} {:answer/id #uuid "8661e5d4-9e46-5bb8-9d5d-b459bbbbafb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,1,-2⟩=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648905}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186648749} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d42046b-7120-5f80-ad85-3af5f483cef6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2405b879-eb0b-5471-82bc-386b75606519" :question/math-academy-id "q-127644" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $Q(1,3,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨-3,1,2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨1,3,-2⟩,$ $n=⟨-3,1,2⟩$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-q)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,1,2⟩ & =⟨1,3,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,1,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,1,2⟩ & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649169 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5f1a083-a0ff-5ef4-8650-1de2eb7e2811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648983} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b1e672-d4ff-52b3-b738-6fbe2a194610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649041}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186648983} :answer-field/id #uuid "03500bac-c525-52f4-9a66-125fc773558e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649067} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0572395e-8bd7-522a-8aac-e065c2982fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648762} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5ac74b-0536-573a-a296-57b6cb6d6538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649036}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186648762} :answer-field/id #uuid "0edaaf78-cf68-5dc5-8e7d-aadad7e0bf9a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649071} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c497e92-d06b-5ceb-af0d-1727293ff002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648813} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef85052-a173-5311-9d31-440e0c1a2d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186648813} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ead99b9-0c69-57c6-8835-1be18e83f0d4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649089} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40f6df07-fcb5-5e7d-98d2-223cec6aa2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648833} {:answer/id #uuid "ca16acc2-4e86-5d99-84fd-c01237c2bbb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648989}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186648833} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca4aa45b-ef89-58dc-b513-4f6865e813eb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27b245f8-4432-5376-a696-6e071e6e8d4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-181792" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d1a3cae836bcc899355c477383b6a5a6173185f9e16d4aa4af94d7ce6a191e0.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2\\,{{field-1}}\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,5,\\,1\\,{{field-3}}\\,y\\,{{field-4}}\\,5\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2<x<5,\\,1<y<5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we also need to\n\n- *include* the left edge ($x=2$),\n\n- *include* the right edge ($x=5$), and\n\n- *include* the bottom edge ($y=1$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbols to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\le x\\le 5,\\,1\\le y<5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 5,\\,1\\le y<5\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a2867cd-42cd-521a-87d7-40866c327787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,0,1⟩=⟨-8,3,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648771} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5396aa-2000-50eb-b56f-d29c1833169e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-8,3,8⟩=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648777} {:answer/id #uuid "b133e52e-7390-54ff-953f-45dbb2bc2465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-8,3,8⟩=⟨-1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648944} {:answer/id #uuid "b583a85c-7695-5aae-8e5e-5c2e1546e388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-8,3,8⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648951} {:answer/id #uuid "c48d643f-d5ac-505e-a27b-524e328f39e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,0,1⟩=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648979}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186648777} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3017941-3772-5afa-ab59-40b7bed44e82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "31899082-f33c-5f66-9e2a-99916a98d704" :question/math-academy-id "q-33034" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point  $Q(-1,0,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1-8t \\\\ y &=2+3t \\\\ z &=-2+8t.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨-1,0,1⟩.$  Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1-8t \\\\ y &=2+3t \\\\ z &=-2+8t\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩+t\\text{ }\\underbrace{⟨-8,\\,3,\\,8⟩}_{v}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-8,\\,3,\\,8⟩,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-8,\\,3,\\,8⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-8,3,8⟩ & =⟨-1,0,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-8,3,8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-8,3,8⟩ & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3635bd05-fae2-5f09-986c-2cbc48b55aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648823} {:answer/id #uuid "42b055b3-eae6-5a25-9608-2b88563fc9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648836} {:answer/id #uuid "747da058-ab74-5858-a5cb-6d7d6db4f4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648877} {:answer/id #uuid "7a94802f-eaff-513a-a320-c2d7be7264f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648887} {:answer/id #uuid "eb98dcd4-c5f3-57b2-b421-be9eff63fdb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649034}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186648823} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2f6b601-44a5-53b5-9e12-fd3a02bd84ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "972c3fb4-abad-5f3d-9069-eac67c5f744a" :question/math-academy-id "q-70863" :question/problem "A nonzero vector $⟨a,\\,b,\\,0⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨2,-4,6⟩=16.$ What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨2,-4,6⟩=16$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨2,-4,6⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-4,6⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2{t}_{1}-4{t}_{2}+6{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ 2{t}_{1} & =4{t}_{2}-6{t}_{3} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =2{t}_{2}-3{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨2{t}_{2}-3{t}_{3},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{2},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the third component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{3}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{2}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{2}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨2,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{b}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54f5c6c3-46de-5ff2-872c-f71a46587a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648850} {:answer/id #uuid "74181530-763d-5752-9aae-f7073f61d688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648876} {:answer/id #uuid "a096057c-c6b4-552c-8970-eff16a363103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648928} {:answer/id #uuid "ad4c1250-fbba-54bd-b5cc-e4d52e4b431e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648939} {:answer/id #uuid "d30f61e3-eac2-5305-9d89-4c56f46192d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186648876} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1deb82b-9230-5b85-b53b-dae83f1d0ae0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9eeca00c-920d-5040-aedc-0a5393549ad9" :question/math-academy-id "q-127883" :question/problem "A nonzero vector $⟨0,\\,b,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨3,-1,1⟩=11.$ What is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨3,-1,1⟩=11$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨3,-1,1⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-1,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3{t}_{1}-{t}_{2}+{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =3{t}_{1}+{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},\\,3{t}_{1}+{t}_{3},\\,{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{3}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{3}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16651328-5ec0-57ed-be2b-19d09f656b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648786} {:answer/id #uuid "c4024875-b1ce-53cf-a46d-af84d6cbf5e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186648978} {:answer/id #uuid "e082ce6e-31cf-5712-b73e-b916743395d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649019} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e99139-db8f-5436-8ae3-34c32656e723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649029} {:answer/id #uuid "f02621a6-c271-54fe-88ff-9b44053537fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649019} :answer-field/id #uuid "1af28a74-6462-5490-ba14-5687b9bb3888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6ca9c4b-f55e-5e8b-a385-67405fa9061e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127646" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-3,7)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of $P$ is $p=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $n=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ is normal to the plane. \n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b22bc88-8926-5ec5-b895-ad7b170e8898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650561} {:answer/id #uuid "562455e0-7e89-5ea6-89dd-7d8311804ebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650572} {:answer/id #uuid "b9992c06-623a-5a8d-a2d3-57f824b366bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650741} {:answer/id #uuid "ce35ff1a-8772-540e-9cfb-6d091d211702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650768} {:answer/id #uuid "d37d0741-adc1-5af7-8aa4-54e04a5b3806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650778}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650561} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b253e68-800a-55f5-a60e-25190f8caf84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c417f559-8a4c-5ed3-9987-51de68d51181" :question/math-academy-id "q-33318" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point  $P(1,3,1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of $P$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $n=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32f6c42d-eddc-5dce-9e9a-d67b9af4acbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-19,25⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660513} {:answer/id #uuid "502a79cc-0d1e-568e-9432-e8a62a1ff773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-19,-16⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660551} {:answer/id #uuid "7baedfad-e1e4-5df4-8f6f-10cc7740650c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,25,9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660603} {:answer/id #uuid "b026c3bc-c88e-54d5-8029-4b4ab3939d87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨13,0,-7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660671} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ec61d5-e525-5cc0-9e0e-6910712f33bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,19,-25⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660754}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660754} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4d1bfe4-031e-5af7-94e2-795a527b99c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3d1133f-829b-534e-b655-fa677f60efbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-19770" :question/problem "Find $(3a+2b)\\times c$, if $a\\times c=⟨0,1,-3⟩$ and $b\\times c=⟨0,8,-8⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use distributive property:\n\n$$\n(3a+2b)\\times c=(3a)\\times c+(2b)\\times c\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n  \n\n$$\n(3a)\\times c+(2b)\\times c=3(a\\times c)+2(b\\times c)\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $a\\times c$ and $b\\times c,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(a\\times c)+2(b\\times c) & =3⟨0,1,-3⟩+2⟨0,8,-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,3,-9⟩+⟨0,16,-16⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,19,-25⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20903be6-89e3-5119-ab2f-761f0d0da1c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660494}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660494} :answer-field/id #uuid "1465d378-1afb-5561-9949-861532da7c31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660774} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2d057c7-4c76-57ee-ad78-fa96cae384aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660675}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660675} :answer-field/id #uuid "9172dc51-97c6-5bfc-9571-88a20012b26e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccbaa794-afb5-5200-b91c-e8c3a1f38e0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-280352" :question/problem "Let $c\\times a=⟨1,5⟩$ and $c\\times b=⟨-2,4⟩.$ Find $c\\times (2a-3b).$\n\n$c\\times (2a-3b)=⟨$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\nc\\times (2a-3b)=c\\times (2a)-c\\times (3b)\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\nc\\times (2a)-c\\times (3b)=2(c\\times a)-3(c\\times b)\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $c\\times a$ and $c\\times b,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(c\\times a)-3(c\\times b) & =2⟨1,5⟩-3⟨-2,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,10⟩+⟨6,-12⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c70946-dfe7-554b-9007-30256b8c2ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18i-12j+30k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660472} {:answer/id #uuid "592fd51c-9b3c-5bd3-816a-b739c759e2c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12i-4j-20k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660559} {:answer/id #uuid "709826a9-7b4d-53fc-b05e-86e3d62e554c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6i+2j+10k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660592} {:answer/id #uuid "af916b0a-dcf4-51e3-854e-766f2b0af786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i-j-5k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660668} {:answer/id #uuid "c990af44-29d2-5d3d-8694-b32fa30537b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6i-2j-10k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660694}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660559} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8a1ec7c-be3c-5d51-93a5-b3a8335ca9d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef9d2387-b7ae-53f7-b8c6-1610d51dc482" :question/math-academy-id "q-216352" :question/problem "Find $(4a-5b)\\times b$, if $b\\times a=-3i+j+5k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\na\\times a=0.\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =-b\\times a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n(4a-5b)\\times b=(4a)\\times b-(5b)\\times b\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n(4a)\\times b-(5b)\\times b=4(a\\times b)-5(b\\times b)\n$$\n\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $b\\times b=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(a\\times b)-5(b\\times b) & =4(-b\\times a)-5(0) \\\\  & =-4(b\\times a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substitute the given value for $b\\times a$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4(b\\times a) & =-4(-3i+j+5k) \\\\  & =12i-4j-20k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0399bfd4-dfd2-5b82-8803-34a6ced6339e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\mathbf i+7\\mathbf j+\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186711103} {:answer/id #uuid "8593a606-a20d-5545-8be3-1556e099e38a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\mathbf i+3\\mathbf j+5\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186711110} {:answer/id #uuid "ae50beda-c4e4-5d7d-b6ea-1f6cf995cf8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\mathbf i+5\\mathbf j+3\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186711112} {:answer/id #uuid "aea1cc39-9211-59f9-b9c0-177486f0636a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j+5\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186711113} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d889de-75d8-5ca7-85a1-af5857ef7a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\mathbf i+4\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186711116}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711110} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f3503e-368c-50ec-8366-cbf1b93030f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186711104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55262969-4b93-56e8-8ac6-8050498b3bd0" :question/problem "Find $(2\\mathbf a-\\mathbf b)\\times(\\mathbf c+2\\mathbf d)$, given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf c&=-\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j+\\mathbf k, &\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf d&=\\mathbf j,\\\\\n\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c&=-\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j-\\mathbf k, &\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf d&=\\mathbf j-\\mathbf k.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Distribute over both parentheses. Every term from the first factor must be crossed with every term from the second, in that order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2\\mathbf a-\\mathbf b)\\times(\\mathbf c+2\\mathbf d)\n&=(2\\mathbf a)\\times\\mathbf c+(2\\mathbf a)\\times(2\\mathbf d)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c-\\mathbf b\\times(2\\mathbf d)\\\\\n&=2(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf c)+4(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf d)\\\\\n&\\quad-(\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c)-2(\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf d).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute all four given products and combine components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&2(-\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j+\\mathbf k)+4\\mathbf j\\\\\n&\\quad-(-\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j-\\mathbf k)-2(\\mathbf j-\\mathbf k)\\\\\n&=(-2+1)\\mathbf i+(2+4-1-2)\\mathbf j\\\\\n&\\quad+(2+1+2)\\mathbf k\\\\\n&=-\\mathbf i+3\\mathbf j+5\\mathbf k.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth terms involving $\\mathbf b$ retain the minus sign from the first parenthesis."}]}
